**What and Why:**
Inheritance lets a child class reuse a parent's code and override specific parts. This is how Django's class-based views work — `APIView`, `ModelViewSet` are parent classes, you inherit from them and override only what's different. Without inheritance you'd rewrite same code in every class — that's what inheritance solves.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: BASIC INHERITANCE — PARENT AND CHILD
# ============================================================

class Animal:  # PARENT CLASS — contains common code shared by all animals
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        # parent __init__ saves name on every animal object
        # Dog, Cat, Bird all need a name — so it lives in parent

    def sound(self):
        print("Some generic animal sound")
        # default sound — used when child doesn't override this method
        # if child overrides it, parent's version is ignored for that child

    def info(self):
        print(f"{self.name} is an animal")
        # this method is NOT overridden in any child
        # so ALL children inherit and use this exact method
        # no need to rewrite in Dog, Cat, Bird — that's the power of inheritance


class Dog(Animal):  # Dog INHERITS from Animal
    # Dog(Animal) means: Dog is a child of Animal
    # Dog automatically gets everything Animal has — __init__, sound(), info()

    def sound(self):
        # OVERRIDING parent's sound() method
        # Dog has its own sound — replaces parent's generic sound
        # Python checks child first — if found, uses child's version
        # if not found in child, goes to parent
        print(f"{self.name} says Woof!")
        # self.name works here because Dog inherited __init__ from Animal
        # self.name was saved in Animal's __init__ — Dog can access it


class Cat(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        # Cat overrides sound() with its own version
        print(f"{self.name} says Meow!")


class Bird(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print(f"{self.name} says Tweet!")


# ============================================================
# CREATING OBJECTS AND TESTING
# ============================================================

d1 = Dog("Rex")
c1 = Cat("Whiskers")
b1 = Bird("Kiwi")
a1 = Animal("Generic")  # plain Animal object — no override

d1.sound()   # Rex says Woof! — Dog's own version
c1.sound()   # Whiskers says Meow! — Cat's own version
b1.sound()   # Kiwi says Tweet! — Bird's own version
a1.sound()   # Some generic animal sound — parent's version

# info() not overridden anywhere — all use parent's version
d1.info()    # Rex is an animal
c1.info()    # Whiskers is an animal
b1.info()    # Kiwi is an animal
# same method, different objects, different name printed
```

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 2: POLYMORPHISM WITH INHERITANCE
# ============================================================

# polymorphism = same method name, different behaviour per object
# inheritance makes polymorphism possible

animals = [Dog("Rex"), Cat("Whiskers"), Bird("Kiwi"), Animal("Generic")]

for animal in animals:
    animal.sound()
    # same .sound() call on every object
    # each object handles it differently based on its own class
    # Dog → Woof, Cat → Meow, Bird → Tweet, Animal → generic
    # no if/else needed — each class knows its own behaviour

# Rex says Woof!
# Whiskers says Meow!
# Kiwi says Tweet!
# Some generic animal sound
```

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 3: WHAT HAPPENS WITHOUT OVERRIDE
# ============================================================

class Fish(Animal):
    pass
    # pass = empty class body — Fish inherits everything from Animal
    # no sound() override means Fish uses Animal's sound()

f1 = Fish("Nemo")
f1.sound()   # Some generic animal sound — falls back to parent
f1.info()    # Nemo is an animal — inherited from parent

# this shows Python's lookup order:
# 1. Look in child class first
# 2. If not found, look in parent class
# 3. If not found in parent, look in grandparent (if exists)
```

